WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.193
Let's spend a few minutes going over how to design slash commands that actually work well in

2
00:00:05.193 --> 00:00:06.943
practice, not just technically.

3
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:13.000
We'll cover the core design principle of narrow scope, how to write an effective command prompt,

4
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:17.000
when to use freeform arguments versus a structured template,

5
00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:23.000
and how teams and enterprises distribute commands so everyone has access.

6
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:27.000
Every command you build forces the same set of decisions,

7
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:31.000
how much it should do, what to call it, and how it reaches your teammates.

8
00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:35.000
Narrow scope command means a command designed to do exactly one thing,

9
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:39.000
the single responsibility principle applied to slash commands.

10
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:44.000
Verb-noun convention is the naming pattern you'll see in the best command libraries,

11
00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:48.000
slash review-pr, slash run-tests, slash explain-file.

12
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:52.000
The verb states the action and the noun states the target.

13
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:58.000
Someone new to the team can read the keyword and know what the command does before they ever open the file.

14
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:02.000
Managed settings is the organization-enforced tier you already met,

15
00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:06.000
the one with the highest precedence that nobody overrides locally.

16
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:13.000
It carries commands too, so admins push them to every user in an organization automatically.

17
00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:18.000
Each of these connects to a concrete decision you'll make when building a command library.

18
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:22.000
The number one design rule for slash commands is narrow scope.

19
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:29.000
A command that does one thing is predictable, easy to maintain, and composes well with other commands.

20
00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:35.000
A command that does many things, say, it reviews the pull request, runs the test suite,

21
00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:41.000
and suggests a deployment strategy all in one go is a problem waiting to happen.

22
00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:44.000
The instruction template becomes a multi-paragraph tangle.

23
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:48.000
Claude has conflicting signals about what to prioritize.

24
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:51.000
Users don't know what output to expect.

25
00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:56.000
And when something goes wrong, it's hard to tell which part of the template caused it.

26
00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:01.000
Build small, focused commands. Compose them when you need to cover more ground.

27
00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:04.000
Every command file is a system prompt.

28
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:09.000
Four elements make a system prompt effective when it's used as a reusable command.

29
00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:13.000
First, role. Tell Claude what perspective it's operating from.

30
00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:16.000
You are a senior engineer reviewing for security issues.

31
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:23.000
Second, task. Describe exactly what to do. Review the diff passed as args.

32
00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:28.000
Third, output format. Specify how the response should be structured.

33
00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:34.000
Return findings as a bulleted list, grouped by severity. Critical, high, low.

34
00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:40.000
Fourth, constraints. What to exclude, what tone to use. Any scope limits.

35
00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:45.000
Missing any of these leads to output that varies unpredictably across runs,

36
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:49.000
which defeats the entire point of having a reusable command.

37
00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:53.000
The choice between freeform args and a structured template

38
00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:56.000
comes down to how much shape the input needs.

39
00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:00.000
Freeform works perfectly when a single piece of text is all you need.

40
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:05.000
Slash explainfileapp.py passes one file name.

41
00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:08.000
Clean, low friction, no setup.

42
00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:12.000
A structured template is better when input has multiple fields,

43
00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:15.000
or when getting the shape wrong would cause errors.

44
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:20.000
In that case, write the template to explicitly prompt for each piece.

45
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:25.000
Provide your PR number, reviewer role, and primary focus area.

46
00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:28.000
Users follow the template rather than guessing.

47
00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:32.000
The overhead of structure pays off when the command is complex enough

48
00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:37.000
that incorrect input would produce meaningless or misleading output.

49
00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:39.000
Naming conventions matter more than they seem.

50
00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:43.000
The verb-noun pattern, slash review-pr, slash run-tests,

51
00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:47.000
slash explainfile, slash generate-changelog,

52
00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:50.000
is the standard because it's self-documenting.

53
00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:54.000
Anyone reading the command list can infer what each command does

54
00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:56.000
without opening a single file.

55
00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:01.000
Contrast that with slash-pr, slash-code, or something worse like slash-do-it.

56
00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:03.000
Those names tell you nothing.

57
00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:06.000
You have to read the file to understand what the command does,

58
00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:09.000
and even then, you might not be sure.

59
00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:12.000
A consistent naming convention across a team's command library

60
00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:14.000
creates a shared vocabulary.

61
00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:17.000
New teammates can guess the right keyword

62
00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:19.000
before they've even read the documentation,

63
00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:22.000
and that's exactly the outcome you want.

64
00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:27.000
Team distribution for project-scoped commands is essentially free.

65
00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:31.000
You commit .clod slash commands to version control as part of the project,

66
00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:35.000
and from that point forward, every developer who clones or pulls the repo

67
00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:37.000
has the full command library.

68
00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:39.000
Updates work the same way.

69
00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:41.000
Improve a command, commit,

70
00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:44.000
and the update propagates via normal pull and merge.

71
00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:49.000
There's no separate sharing mechanism, no configuration management layer,

72
00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:52.000
no documentation to update about where the files live.

73
00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:55.000
The commands travel with the codebase.

74
00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:59.000
This is one of the most underappreciated properties of the project-level scope.

75
00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:01.000
The distribution channel already exists.

76
00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:03.000
You're just using it.

77
00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:06.000
For organizations running clod code at scale,

78
00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:10.000
managed settings provide a centralized distribution mechanism.

79
00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:13.000
Administrators configure commands at the organization level,

80
00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:18.000
and those commands are pushed automatically to every user in the organization.

81
00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:20.000
Users don't need to do anything.

82
00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:23.000
They can't accidentally delete org-level commands

83
00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:25.000
or override them with personal files.

84
00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:29.000
This matters most for commands that enforce compliance workflows,

85
00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:33.000
or organization-wide automation standards.

86
00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:37.000
When you want to guarantee that every developer in a 1,000-person org

87
00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:41.000
has access to the same slash review for compliance command,

88
00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:43.000
managed settings is how you do it

89
00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:47.000
without relying on every team to maintain their own copy.

90
00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:52.000
Two anti-patterns come up constantly when teams start building command libraries.

91
00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:55.000
The first is the overly broad command.

92
00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:57.000
Someone builds slash do-everything

93
00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:01.000
that reviews, refactors, tests, and deploys in one file.

94
00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:04.000
It works occasionally and fails confusingly.

95
00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:06.000
Narrow the scope.

96
00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:12.000
The second anti-pattern is duplicating clod.md instructions inside command files.

97
00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:17.000
clod.md already provides standing context at the session level.

98
00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:19.000
If your command file copies those instructions,

99
00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:21.000
you've created maintenance debt.

100
00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:25.000
Update clod.md later and forget to update the command,

101
00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:27.000
and now they're out of sync.

102
00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:32.000
Commands should extend and build on what clod.md establishes, not repeat it.

103
00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:36.000
These two patterns, overbroad scope and instruction duplication,

104
00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:40.000
are the most common ways command libraries become unreliable.

105
00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:44.000
Effective slash commands keep a narrow scope,

106
00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:46.000
use verb-noun naming,

107
00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:50.000
and reach the team through version control or managed settings.

108
00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:55.000
Freeform arguments works when a single piece of text is all you need,

109
00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:57.000
and a structured template earns its keep

110
00:06:57.000 --> 00:07:01.000
when the input has shape and getting it wrong would cost you.

111
00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:04.000
Avoid the command that tries to do everything

112
00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:08.000
and avoid copying clod.md instructions into a command file

113
00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:11.000
where they'll quietly drift apart.

114
00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:12.000
That's it for this lesson.

115
00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:14.000
I'll see you in the next one.

